                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I had mixed feelings about the game, but at the end I decided to write this review as a positive one, despite the many caveats. They are not gamebreaking (unless you count broken achievements), but being better informed is the best. \n\nMagibot is a cute and quite simple puzzle game. It looks like it's been ported from mobile devices, but actually the PC version came a year before it has been ported to android and iphone/ipad.  ( The 13€ game costs 3-4€ on mobiles, it's a different market)\n\nPositives of the game:  quite a lot, actually. Objectively it's a good, solid product, and it works well from start until finish.\nIt's appealing both in gameplay, and in looks.  Could have a tad faster movement/game speed, but otherwise it overall has good qualities to it. Music is nice, it tries to tell a story and kinda succeeds at it so it's not just puzzle levels.\n\nNegatives, the mentioned caveats: Pet peeves, inconsistencies and problems. Annoying or dealbreaking, your mileage may vary. \n-First of all: this is a PC game, and someone in 2017 thought it's a good idea to use wasd, mouse and Enter at the same time. Why not F instead of Enter? There are so many buttons around wasd that would allow comfy play.  ( Also, no way to rebind controls)\n-It's likely engine-side, but when placing the magic circles, the game treats clicking and dragging completely illogically. If you drag one circle, your next click will place one. If you click on the \"stash\" of circles, you just drop one of them immediately there. If you want to delete, you have to click the delete button and then the circles.\n- Platform surfaces are misaligned. You can stuck just below the top horizontal side of any platform when wind pushes you up,  because it overreaches the vertical one, leading to numerous deaths and repeat. ( Imagine it as the letter T) I experienced this at least half a dozen times in one run, in about 4 hours! Why no tester pointed this out? Likely it's super easy to fix.\n-If testers were mentioned - some levels feature some kind of storm chasing you, having a pretty scary darkness-tendrils-rocks flying feel to them. That, coupled with the snow levels' snowing effect makes that chapter visually super noisy. And for some reason, the storm's darkness only affects the surface items placed on levels ( climbable walls, surface you're running on, surface of walls) - but not the bulk of the walls, and the \"outside of the game\" areas. [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2062943712]It's visually jarring to see those straight lines [/url] where the filter stops being applied. Nobody noticed it while in development? :|\n- The ingame cube-counter - for some reason - shows half of the collected cubes. ( I have 43 ingame, 86 at the save slots screen). I stopped caring about them after it turned out that:\n-Most of the progress  achievements are broken. % completion, areas visited, cubes collected - just doesn't work after reaching a specific point. I got \"The Rage\" around the end of the game, but that's all, nothing else since-around the 25% completion one. ( Say hi to the people using SAM to unlock everything)\n\nSo, basically that's all. Game is cute, game is nice, but somehow nothing got the same care the core graphics got. General looks, gameplay, controls, steam implementation - these are all problematic, scaling from messy to outright not working.",
